,"g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":48.424862,"lo":-123.36553,"d":2006},{"n":"Ogden Point","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.416667,"lo":-123.389889,"d":37,"ds":"Ogden Point is a deep water port facility located in the southwestern corner of the city of Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. Its advantageous geographic location on the southern tip of Vancouver Island, by the Strait of Juan de Fuca, close to major population centres Vancouver and Seattle, has made it an attractive cruise ship destination.","wpu":"Ogden Point","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Ogden_Point_Breakwater_in_Victoria_at_dawn-01.jpg/960px-Ogden_Point_Breakwater_in_Victoria_at_dawn-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Victoria Harbour (British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.421111,"lo":-123.392778,"d":500,"ds":"Victoria Harbour is a harbour, seaport, and seaplane airport in the Canadian city of Victoria, British Columbia. It serves as a cruise ship and ferry destination for tourists and visitors to the city and Vancouver Island. It is both a port of entry and an airport of entry for general aviation.","wpu":"Victoria Harbour (British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Aerial_photo_of_Victoria%2C_BC%2C_on_Vancouver_Island%2C_Canada.jpg/960px-Aerial_photo_of_Victoria%2C_BC%2C_on_Vancouver_Island%2C_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Immigration Building","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.421,"lo":-123.3847,"d":592,"ds":"The Immigration Building, also known as Dominion Immigration House, was located at the corner of Ontario Street and Dallas Road in the James Bay neighbourhood of Victoria, British Columbia. Between 1909 and 1957, thousands of immigrants to Canada were detained and processed in the building, which closed in 1958 and was demolished in 1978.","wpu":"Immigration Building","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Immigration_Detention_Hospital_in_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_1909.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"British Columbia Law Enforcement Memorial","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4187,"lo":-123.3699,"d":1495,"ds":"The British Columbia Law Enforcement Memorial is a memorial commemorating law enforcement professionals who died in the line of duty, in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. It was unveiled by then-Premier of British Columbia Gordon Campbell on 26 September 2004, and sits in the grounds of the British Columbia Parliament Buildings.","wpu":"British Columbia Law Enforcement Memorial","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/British_Columbia_Law_Enforcement_Memorial%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_07.jpg/960px-British_Columbia_Law_Enforcement_Memorial%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Front Fountain","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.42016,"lo":-123.37006,"d":1512,"ds":"Front Fountain is a fountain designed by Hooper & Watkins, installed outside the British Columbia Parliament Buildings, in Victoria, British Columbia. It was manufactured in 1905, by Joseph W. Fiske in New York, and installed in 1906. The fountain has one large ceramic tile basin with a granite rim.","wpu":"Front Fountain","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/BC_Parliament_fountain_-_2970416625.jpg/960px-BC_Parliament_fountain_-_2970416625.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Emily Carr House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4138,"lo":-123.37,"d":1518,"ds":"Emily Carr House (also known as Carr House) is a National Historic Site located in Victoria, British Columbia. It was the childhood home of Canadian painter Emily Carr, and had a lasting impression on her paintings and writings.","wpu":"Emily Carr House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Carr_House_1863.jpg/960px-Carr_House_1863.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Netherlands Centennial Carillon","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.42043,"lo":-123.36848,"d":1633,"ds":"The Netherlands Centennial Carillon is a 62-bell carillon located in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. Its tower is located at the intersection of Government Street and Belleville Street, in front of the Royal British Columbia Museum and across the street from the Parliament Building.","wpu":"Netherlands Centennial Carillon","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/July_2008_Victoria_Carillon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Statue of James Cook (Victoria, British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.42174,"lo":-123.3688,"d":1650,"ds":"A statue of Captain James Cook stood in Victoria, British Columbia, from 1976 until 2021, when it was toppled in a protest. It was a fibreglass copy of a bronze statue of 1912 by John Tweed in Whitby, Yorkshire, England. The Victoria Environmental Enhancement Foundation commissioned the work from Derek and Patricia Freeborn to mark the 200th anniversary of Cook's departure on his third voyage in 1776.","wpu":"Statue of James Cook (Victoria, British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Captain_Cook_Memorial%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada.jpg/960px-Captain_Cook_Memorial%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"100th Anniversary of the Canadian Navy","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4232,"lo":-123.3693,"d":1675,"ds":"100th Anniversary of the Canadian Navy is an outdoor memorial in Victoria, British Columbia. It was constructed to commemorate the founding of the Royal Canadian Navy and opened on the date of the navy's centenary. Its central feature is a bronze statue, sculpted by local artist Nathan Scott, entitled The Homecoming that depicts a Canadian sailor reuniting with his daughter upon his return from deployment.","wpu":"100th Anniversary of the Canadian Navy","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Homecoming_Statue_in_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_01.jpg/960px-Homecoming_Statue_in_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dockside Green","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4294,"lo":-123.3763,"d":1709,"ds":"Dockside Green is a 1,300,000-square-foot (120,000 m2) mixed-use community in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada owned by Vancity Credit Union and noted for its strict adherence to the principles of sustainable architecture or green building. The development sits on 15 acres (6.1 ha) of Victoria's Inner Harbour, and was a brownfield site used by light industry for more than a century.","wpu":"Dockside Green","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Biomass_energy_generator_of_Dockside_Green._READ_INFO_IN_PANORAMIO-COMMENTS_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Biomass_energy_generator_of_Dockside_Green._READ_INFO_IN_PANORAMIO-COMMENTS_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Thunderbird Park (Victoria, British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.42,"lo":-123.3665,"d":1766,"ds":"Thunderbird Park in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada, is next to the Royal British Columbia Museum and home to many totem poles (mostly Gitxsan, Haida, and Kwakwakaʼwakw) and other First Nation monuments. The park takes its name from the mythological Thunderbird of Indigenous North American cultures, which is depicted on many totem poles. Also in the park are St.","wpu":"Thunderbird Park (Victoria, British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Wawadit%27la%28Mungo_Martin_House%29_a_Kwakwaka%27wakw_big_house.jpg/960px-Wawadit%27la%28Mungo_Martin_House%29_a_Kwakwaka%27wakw_big_house.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Odd Fellows Hall (Victoria, British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4252,"lo":-123.3695,"d":1766,"ds":"Odd Fellows Hall is a historic building in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. It is located at the intersection of Wharf Street and Fort Street in downtown Victoria, and it is now the location of The Keg restaurant.","wpu":"Odd Fellows Hall (Victoria, British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Odd_Fellows_Hall%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_22.jpg/960px-Odd_Fellows_Hall%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rogers Building (Victoria, British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.423903,"lo":-123.367892,"d":1803,"ds":"Rogers Building or Rogers Chocolates is a historic building in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada, it is located at 913 Government Street in downtown Victoria. The two-storey building was completed in 1903 by architects Hooper and Watkins for Charles Rogers (1854–1927), a grocer who operated from the opposite side of the street before 1903. Rogers Chocolates continues to operate from this site since.","wpu":"Rogers Building (Victoria, British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Roger%27s_Chocolates%2C_Government_Street%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_05.jpg/960px-Roger%27s_Chocolates%2C_Government_Street%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Victoria (British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.42579,"lo":-123.36851,"d":1862,"ds":"Fort Victoria began as a fur trading post of the Hudson's Bay Company and was the headquarters of HBC operations in the Columbia District, a large fur trading area now part of the province of British Columbia, Canada and the U.S. state of Washington. Construction of Fort Victoria in 1843 highlighted the beginning of a permanent British settlement now known as Victoria, the capital city of British Columbia.","wpu":"Fort Victoria (British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Fort_Victoria_circa_1860.jpg/960px-Fort_Victoria_circa_1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mark Loria Gallery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4247,"lo":-123.3672,"d":1887,"ds":"Mark Loria Gallery (formerly Alcheringa Gallery) is a Canadian contemporary art gallery specializing in Northwest Coast, Coast Salish, and Canadian indigenous fine art. The gallery exhibits artwork from the contemporary period (1960s forward) as well as representing over 60 current artists.","wpu":"Mark Loria Gallery","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Mark_Loria_Gallery_02.jpg/960px-Mark_Loria_Gallery_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Morley's Soda Water Factory","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4274,"lo":-123.3694,"d":1910,"ds":"Morley's Soda Water Factory is an historic building in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. It is one of the few industrial buildings in the Old Town District to survive the 1880s.","wpu":"Morley's Soda Water Factory","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Morley%27s_Soda_Water_Factory%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_24.jpg/960px-Morley%27s_Soda_Water_Factory%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_24.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Andrew's Presbyterian Church (Victoria, British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4238,"lo":-123.366,"d":1925,"ds":"St. Andrew's Presbyterian Church is a Presbyterian church in downtown Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. The congregation was founded in 1862 and the current church building was completed in 1890.","wpu":"St. Andrew's Presbyterian Church (Victoria, British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/St._Andrew%27s_Presbyterian_Church%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_06.jpg/960px-St._Andrew%27s_Presbyterian_Church%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Church of Our Lord (Victoria, British Columbia)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.42,"lo":-123.364,"d":1947,"ds":"The Church of Our Lord, built in 1866-1870 and is located at 626 Blanshard Street in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada, is an historic Carpenter Gothic church that is designated as a National Historic Site of Canada. It was affiliated with the Reformed Episcopal Church at its beginning, which became a member of the Anglican Church in North America, upon its creation in 2009.","wpu":"Church of Our Lord (Victoria, British Columbia)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Church_of_Our_Lord_%28Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%292.JPG/960px-Church_of_Our_Lord_%28Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%292.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bay Centre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4251,"lo":-123.3661,"d":1980,"ds":"Bay Centre (formerly the Victoria Eaton Centre) is a shopping mall in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. It is bounded by Douglas, Government, Fort, and View streets, in the city's historic centre. It has 39,115 square metres (421,030 sq ft) of retail space.","wpu":"Bay Centre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/The_Bay_Centre%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_09.jpg/960px-The_Bay_Centre%2C_Victoria%2C_British_Columbia%2C_Canada_09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Beacon Hill Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.412,"lo":-123.364,"d":1997,"ds":"Beacon Hill Park is a 75 ha (190 acres) park located along the shore of Juan de Fuca Strait in Victoria, British Columbia that is popular both with tourists and locals. The park contains a number of amenities including woodland and shoreline trails, two playgrounds, a waterpark, playing fields, a children's farm, tennis and pickleball courts, many ponds, and landscaped gardens.","wpu":"Beacon Hill Park","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Canada_IMG_5640_%28211348807%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Central Branch of Greater Victoria Public Library","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.4233,"lo":-123.3642,"d":2028,"ds":"The Central Branch of the Greater Victoria Public Library (GVPL) is the system's main branch, located at 735 Broughton Street in the downtown core of Victoria, British Columbia, Canada. The Heritage Room includes a special collection with approximately 2,500 items related to the history of Vancouver Island.","wpu":"Central Branch of Greater Victoria Public Library","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Victoria%2C_B.C._%282012%29_-_77.JPG/960px-Victoria%2C_B.C._%282012%29_-_77.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Esquimalt","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.432276,"lo":-123.405844,"d":2062,"ds":"The Township of Esquimalt () is a municipality at the southern tip of Vancouver Island, in British Columbia, Canada.","wpu":"Esquimalt","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Esquimalt_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Esquimalt_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Point Ellice Bridge disaster","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":48.434256,"lo":-123.378094,"d":2110,"ds":"The Point Ellice Bridge disaster occurred on May 26, 1896, in Victoria, British Columbia, when a streetcar crowded with 143 passengers on their way to attend celebrations of Queen Victoria's birthday crashed through Point Ellice Bridge into the Upper Harbour. 55 people were killed in the accident, making it one of the worst transit disasters in British Columbia.","wpu":"Point Ellice Bridge disaster","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Point_Ellice_Bridge_Disaster_Victoria.jpg/960px-Point_Ellice_Bridge_Disaster_Victoria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Victoria is the capital city of the Canadian province of British Columbia, located on the southern tip of Vancouver Island off Canada's Pacific coast. The city has a population of 91,867, and the Greater Victoria area has a population of 397,237. The city of Victoria is the seventh most densely populated city in Canada with 4,406 inhabitants per square kilometre (11,410/sq mi).","wpu":"Victoria, British Columbia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Victoria_BC%2C_Canada_%28Johnson_Street%29_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Victoria_BC%2C_Canada_%28Johnson_Street%29_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}